raw_findings.attack_scenario:
Attacker knows a target address (Tetrac mints deterministic `{telegramId}@telegram.tetrac.xyz` addresses — see tests/rate-limit-sustained-lockout.test.ts:11). For each passkey guess he: (1) POSTs /challenge with a fresh case-permutation of the victim's email (server normalizes, resolves the victim's account, returns a live challenge — proven below), (2) derives appKey = PBKDF2(guess, SHA-256(appId:normalizedEmail)) and the ed25519 auth key locally, (3) POSTs /login with that permutation + signature. Every ~10 guesses he rotates to a new casing and the counter starts at zero again; he never sees a 429. I proved this end-to-end against the shipped dist with maxAttempts=2:

  register: 201  (email victim@example.com)
  victim@example.com -> 200 {"challenge":"3fcc3282…"}
  VICTIM@EXAMPLE.COM -> 200 {"challenge":"c408ea9a…"}
  ViCtIm@ExAmPlE.cOm -> 200 {"challenge":"ccccc287…"}
  nobody@example.com -> 400 {"error":"publicKey or email required"}   <-- proves the variants really hit the registered account
  burn victim@: 200 / 429 / 429 / 429
  variant still allowed: 200                                          <-- VicTim@example.com, fresh budget

and on /login: `same case: 401 401 429` then `Victim@example.com 401 401 429`, `vIctim@example.com 401 401 429`, `VICTIM@EXAMPLE.COM 401 401 429` — every casing gets its own full budget.

