raw_findings.attack_scenario:
Attacker gets XSS (or a malicious browser extension content script, or brief access to an unlocked device) on an app whose users registered via `registerWithBiometric` on a non-PRF authenticator (Windows Hello on older builds, most Android platform authenticators pre-PRF, Safari < 18). The injected script runs, with no user interaction and no Touch ID prompt, while the vault is auto-locked and the user is away:
  const db = await new Promise(r => { const q = indexedDB.open('ttc_passkey_store', 2); q.onsuccess = () => r(q.result); });
  const rec = await new Promise(r => { const q = db.transaction('gate_secrets','readonly').objectStore('gate_secrets').get(credId); q.onsuccess = () => r(q.result); });
  const appKey = hex(await crypto.subtle.decrypt({name:'AES-GCM', iv: rec.iv}, rec.cryptoKey, rec.ciphertext));
(`credId` is enumerable via `IDBObjectStore.getAllKeys()`, or read from `localStorage['ttc_biometric_unlock']` / `ttc_biometric_reg`.) That value is the account's app key. The attacker then fetches the encrypted wallets with the bearer token that also sits in localStorage (`session.ts:279 getAuthToken`) — or, per the stated threat model, simply reads them from the leaked DB — and decrypts every Solana and EVM private key. Full non-custodial wallet compromise with no biometric ceremony ever performed.

